.\n - Clamp active to [1,128]; fall back to SK_PASS to preserve kernel behavior.\n\n Why it should help:\n - Fewer instructions than a 128-iteration unrolled probe loop.\n - No expensive modulo in the general case (use 32x32->64 multiply-high).\n - Lower I-cache pressure and fewer branches help p99 under high load.\n*/\n //go:build ignore\n\n#include <linux/bpf.h>\n#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_REUSEPORT_SOCKARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 128);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, __u32);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_udp_targets SEC(\".maps\");\n\nstruct agent_state {\n    __u32 active;\n    __u32 pad;\n};\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_ARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 1);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, struct agent_state);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_agent_state SEC(\".maps\");\n\n/* EVOLVE-BLOCK-START\n   Agent UDP reuseport selector (single-shot, de-skewed)\n\n   Problem we saw:\n   - Using reuse->hash directly biased selections to low-index slots when\n     the kernel’s hash occupied a narrow range, starving workers.\n\n   Fix:\n   - First whiten the 32-bit hash with a strong avalanche (Murmur3 fmix32).\n   - Then compute the slot with a fast range reduction:\n       * power-of-two active: bitmask\n       * otherwise: 32x32->64 multiply-high (no expensive modulo)\n   - Keep a single bpf_sk_select_reuseport call; always return SK_PASS.\n\n   Expected effect:\n   - Near-uniform spread across active sockets even if the original hash is\n     biased, improving fairness and reducing per-worker backlog and p99.\n*/\nSEC(\"sk_reuseport/selector\")\nenum sk_action agent_udp_selector(struct sk_reuseport_md *reuse)\n{\n    __u32 key = 0;\n    struct agent_state *st = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&pebble_agent_state, &key);\n    if (!st)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    __u32 active = st->active;\n    if (active == 0)\n        return SK_PASS;\n    if (active > 128)\n        active = 128;\n\n    // Whiten the kernel-provided hash (Murmur3 fmix32).\n    __u32 h = reuse->hash;\n    h ^= h >> 16;\n    h *= 0x85ebca6b;\n    h ^= h >> 13;\n    h *= 0xc2b2ae35;\n    h ^= h >> 16;\n\n    __u32 slot;\n    if ((active & (active - 1)) == 0) {\n        slot = h & (active - 1);\n    } else {\n        __u64 prod = (__u64)h * active;\n        slot = (__u32)(prod >> 32);\n    }\n\n    bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot, 0);\n    return SK_PASS;\n}\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-END\n\nchar _license[] SEC(\"license\") = \"GPL\";\n", "language": "cpp", "parent_id": "de5e5cad-8301-4b2d-a4e5-7380cd87fe04", "generation": 2, "timestamp": 1763053350.2216384, "iteration_found": 3, "metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 5.832], [60000.0, 26.26], [80000.0, 2634.393], [100000.0, 4500.21], [140000.0, 6441.174]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -31694.07, "run_success": 1.0}, "complexity": 0.0, "diversity": 0.0, "metadata": {"changes": "Change 1: Replace 40 lines with 52 lines", "parent_metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 7.168], [60000.0, 2594.76], [80000.0, 2729.137], [100000.0, 660.484], [140000.0, 3815.227]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -18965.904000000002, "run_success": 1.0}, "island": 0}, "prompts": null, "artifacts_json": "{\"perf_expl\": \"**Behavior & Performance**\\n- 50\\u202fk rps saw overall p99 fall to 5.832\\u202fms (parent: 7.168\\u202fms) while the client kept only single-digit outstanding requests even though just workers 0\\u20113 logged traffic, so throughput effectively matched the offered rate (results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:31; results/experiments/exp-20251113-164205/summary.json:31; results/run-20251113-165305/logs/client.log:2; results/run-20251113-165305/logs/server/server.log:4).\\n- At 60\\u202fk rps the whitening step removed the prior low-index bias\\u2014p99 shrank from 2.59\\u202fs to 26\\u202fms despite only workers 1\\u20113 being used, and the client log shows the queue draining every second (results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:51; results/experiments/exp-20251113-164205/summary.json:51; results/run-20251113-165325/logs/server/server.log:4; results/run-20251113-165325/logs/client.log:2).\\n- 80\\u202fk rps regressed: the server emitted just ~57\\u201372\\u202fk responses/sec because traffic still hit only four sockets (0/1/2/5), so each worker spent long stretches in the 10\\u202f\\u00b5s/2\\u202fms busy-wait handlers and queueing drove p99 to 2.63\\u202fs with 23\\u202fk replies missing (results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:70; results/run-20251113-165345/logs/server/server.log:4; results/run-20251113-165345/logs/server/server.log:8; results/run-20251113-165345/logs/client.log:17; internal/server/server.go:286; internal/server/server.go:313).\\n- 100\\u202fk and 140\\u202fk rps collapsed even further\\u2014p99 jumped to 4.5\\u202fs and 6.4\\u202fs versus the parent\\u2019s 0.66\\u202fs and 3.8\\u202fs because only four workers carried ~80\\u201393\\u202fk rps, leaving 111\\u202fk and 356\\u202fk outstanding requests that never came back (results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:90; results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:110; results/experiments/exp-20251113-164205/summary.json:90; results/experiments/exp-20251113-164205/summary.json:110; results/run-20251113-165405/logs/server/server.log:4; results/run-20251113-165405/logs/server/server.log:8; results/run-20251113-165424/logs/server/server.log:4; results/run-20251113-165424/logs/server/server.log:8; results/run-20251113-165405/logs/client.log:17; results/run-20251113-165424/logs/client.log:17).\\n- Confidence: **Medium**\\u2014the logs clearly show under-utilized sockets and accumulating backlogs, but we cannot see why the selector never activates the remaining two reuseport targets.\\n\\n**Program Intent**\\n- The selector\\u2019s stated intent is to whiten `reuse->hash` with a Murmur3 fmix, then pick exactly one slot via bitmask/multiply-high so requests spread evenly with minimal instructions (ebpf/agent.c:41; ebpf/agent.c:73; ebpf/agent.c:81; ebpf/agent.c:89).\\n- The Go server programs the agent state with all six worker FDs, so the policy expects every reuseport socket to participate (internal/server/ebpf.go:103; internal/server/ebpf.go:124).\\n- In practice the behavior only matches that intent at \\u226460\\u202fk rps; at \\u226580\\u202fk rps the selector still feeds only four sockets, causing multi-second p99s and large response loss, so the fairness/latency goals are not achieved (results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:70; results/experiments/exp-20251113-164205/summary.json:90; results/run-20251113-165345/logs/server/server.log:4; results/run-20251113-165405/logs/client.log:17).\\n\\n**Evidence**\\n- Experiment p99 metrics for current and parent policies: `results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:31,51,70,90,110`; `results/experiments/exp-20251113-164205/summary.json:31,51,70,90,110`.\\n- Server worker stats showing only subsets of sockets receive traffic: `results/run-20251113-165305/logs/server/server.log:4`; `results/run-20251113-165325/logs/server/server.log:4`; `results/run-20251113-165345/logs/server/server.log:4`; `results/run-20251113-165405/logs/server/server.log:4`; `results/run-20251113-165424/logs/server/server.log:4`.\\n- Interval throughput shortfalls: `results/run-20251113-165345/logs/server/server.log:8`; `results/run-20251113-165405/logs/server/server.log:8`; `results/run-20251113-165424/logs/server/server.log:8`.\\n- Client backlog/missing-response logs: `res